		</wp:postmeta>
		<wp:postmeta>
			<wp:meta_key><![CDATA[_lb_document_data]]></wp:meta_key>
			<wp:meta_value><![CDATA[{"version":"2.8","root":[{"id":"canvasly-units-logo","type":"image","settings":{"image_url":"https://canvasly.pro/assets/canvasly-logo.png","alt":"Canvasly. Design your pages visually.","link_to":"custom","link":"https://canvasly.pro","link_target":"_self","alignment":"left","width":"120px","height":"96px","object_fit":"contain"},"styles":{"base":{}},"children":[]},{"id":"canvasly-units-wrap","type":"container","settings":{},"styles":{"base":{}},"children":[{"id":"canvasly-units-body","type":"html","settings":{"html":"    <nav class=\"crumbs\" aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\">\n      <ol>\n        <li><a href=\"/canvasly-overview/\">Canvasly</a></li>\n        <li aria-current=\"page\">Units</li>\n      </ol>\n    </nav>\n    <header class=\"page-intro\">\n      <h1>Canvasly units</h1>\n      <p class=\"lede\">Units are the pieces you drag from the left library onto the canvas. Select a unit to edit it in the right panel. That panel uses Content, Style, and Advanced. Pro units appear in the same library when Canvasly Pro is active. Shop units appear only when WooCommerce is active.</p>\n      <p class=\"meta\">Updated <time datetime=\"2026-09-22\">September 22, 2026</time>. Covers Canvasly 0.12.76 and Canvasly Pro 0.6.3.</p>\n    </header>\n\n    <figure>\n      <img src=\"assets/editor-layout.svg\" alt=\"Editor sketch with the unit library on the left, the page canvas in the center, and Content, Style, and Advanced tabs on the right.\" width=\"880\" height=\"420\" decoding=\"async\" fetchpriority=\"high\">\n      <figcaption>Pick a unit on the left, drop it on the canvas, then change it on the right.</figcaption>\n    </figure>\n\n    <article id=\"how-to-add\">\n      <h2>Add a unit</h2>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Open the page with Edit with Canvasly.</li>\n        <li>In the left panel, search or filter by category and click or drag a unit.</li>\n        <li>Drop it into a Container or a Grid, or onto the page.</li>\n        <li>Use the right panel to change the text, image, link, or layout.</li>\n        <li>Save, then publish or update the page in WordPress.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p>Right-click a unit in the library to add or remove it from Favorites. Container cannot be disabled in Units Manager. Other units can be hidden there.</p>\n    </article>\n\n    <article id=\"grid\">\n      <h2>Grid</h2>\n      <figure>\n        <img src=\"assets/grid.svg\" alt=\"A grid sketch with three columns. The bottom-left cell spans two columns. A side panel reads Placement, Column 1, Row 2, 2 by 1, and notes that dragging an edge resizes and neighboring columns share width.\" width=\"880\" height=\"260\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\">\n        <figcaption>Grid places children in rows and columns. A child can span more than one cell.</figcaption>\n      </figure>\n      <p>The Grid unit is a layout that holds other units. Its controls include columns, rows, grid template columns, grid template rows, column gap, row gap, auto flow, align, justify, minimum column and row sizes, auto columns and rows, outline, width, and dense auto-flow. The editor draws a placement grid so you can set column, row, and span. Dragging the left, right, top, or bottom edge resizes the unit, and neighboring columns share the width. A size tip shows while you drag. The Features setting “Grid container” is active by default and covers the same kind of layout on a Container.</p>\n      <p>Usage: add Grid from the layout category, then drop Heading, Image, or Button into the cells. Select a child to set where it sits.</p>\n    </article>\n\n    <article id=\"context-menu\">\n      <h2>Right-click menu</h2>\n      <figure>\n        <img src=\"assets/context-menu.svg\" alt=\"A white menu listing Edit Heading, Duplicate, Copy, Paste, Copy Style, Paste style, Reset style, Save as Template, and Delete.\" width=\"420\" height=\"360\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\">\n        <figcaption>Right-click a unit on the canvas for these actions. Shortcuts can be changed in the editor.</figcaption>\n      </figure>\n      <p>On a unit, the menu offers Edit, Duplicate, Copy, Paste, Copy Style, Paste style, and Reset style. Tabs, Accordion, and Toggle also offer Convert to nested. Container and Grid offer Add New Container and Save as Template. Any unit can be saved as a component. Structure opens the navigator. Delete removes the unit.</p>\n      <p>On empty canvas, the menu offers Paste, Paste All Content, Add New Container, Copy All Content, Delete All Content, and Structure. Copy and paste are kept in the browser so they work across pages and tabs.</p>\n      <p class=\"todo\"><strong>TODO:</strong> The plugin source does not name a “pink tab.” The right-hand settings use the labels Content, Style, and Advanced. The left side is the unit library. If a pink tab appears in your copy of the editor, its purpose is not described in these files.</p>\n    </article>\n\n    <article id=\"wordpress-block\">\n      <h2>WordPress block: Canvasly Template</h2>\n      <p>This is the one WordPress block the plugin registers. Its name in the editor is Canvasly Lite Template, in the Canvasly category. It inserts a saved Canvasly template. It still shows in the normal WordPress editor, where the rest of Canvasly does not load.</p>\n      <table>\n        <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n        <tbody>\n          <tr><td>Template id</td><td>0</td><td>Which saved template to show. 0 means none is chosen.</td></tr>\n        </tbody>\n      </table>\n      <p>The block can be wide or full width, and it supports an HTML anchor and a CSS class. Usage: in the WordPress block editor, add Canvasly Template, then pick the saved template.</p>\n    </article>\n\n    <article id=\"lite-layout\">\n      <h2>Layout units</h2>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-container\">\n        <h3>Container</h3>\n        <p class=\"meta\">Library category: layout. Holds other units.</p>\n        <p>The main box for sections of a page. The readme describes a semantic HTML tag, a link for the whole container, a background overlay, shape dividers, and a self-hosted background video. It cannot be turned off.</p>\n        <p>Usage: add Container, then drop other units inside it.</p>\n      </section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-inner-section\">\n        <h3>Inner Section</h3>\n        <p class=\"meta\">Library category: layout.</p>\n        <p>A nested layout unit registered with Container and Grid.</p>\n        <p>Usage: add Inner Section inside a Container when you need another grouped region.</p>\n      </section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-grid\">\n        <h3>Grid</h3>\n        <p class=\"meta\">Library category: layout. Holds other units.</p>\n        <p>See the Grid section above. Defaults are not a single fixed column count in the short control list; the editor’s grid helper treats three columns when a count is missing.</p>\n      </section>\n    </article>\n\n    <article id=\"lite-basic\">\n      <h2>Basic units</h2>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-heading\"><h3>Heading</h3><p>A title. The readme says it can be linked and can use size presets. Usage: add Heading and type the title in Content.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-text\"><h3>Text Editor</h3><p>Body text. The readme mentions a drop cap and columns. Usage: add Text Editor and write in the text control.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-tinymce\"><h3>TinyMCE Text Editor</h3><p class=\"meta\">Library category: basic.</p><p>A text unit that uses the WordPress visual editor. Usage: add it when you want that editor’s formatting tools.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-button\"><h3>Button</h3><p>A link styled as a button. The readme mentions sizes, icon placement, and alignment. Usage: set the label and the link.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-divider\"><h3>Divider</h3><p>A horizontal rule. The readme mentions patterns and optional text or an icon. Usage: drop it between sections.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-spacer\"><h3>Spacer</h3><p>Empty vertical space. Default height is 40. Usage: set Height.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-icon\"><h3>Icon</h3><p>A single icon from the Canvasly icon library. Usage: pick an icon and, if you want, a link.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-icon-list\"><h3>Icon List</h3><p>A list of items that each have an icon. The readme mentions an inline layout and dividers. Usage: add rows in the list control.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-icon-box\"><h3>Icon Box</h3><p>An icon with a title and text. Controls include icon view, shape, title, text, link, layout, alignment, colors, and hover animation. Usage: choose an icon and write the title.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-progress\"><h3>Progress Bar</h3><p>A labeled bar. Controls include label, value, bar style, percentage, inner text, colors, height, and radius. Usage: set the label and the value.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-counter\"><h3>Counter</h3><p>A number that can count up. The readme says the number, prefix, and suffix update on the canvas while you edit. Usage: set the number and optional prefix or suffix.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-alert\"><h3>Alert</h3><p>A notice. The readme says it can be dismissed. Usage: write the message and choose whether visitors can close it.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-accordion\"><h3>Accordion</h3><p>Stacked panels that open and close. The readme mentions multiple items, icons, and FAQ schema. Usage: add a title and content for each item. Right-click to convert to Nested Accordion.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-toggle\"><h3>Toggle</h3><p>Similar stacked panels. Usage: add items, or convert to Nested Toggle from the right-click menu.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-tabs\"><h3>Tabs</h3><p>A row or column of tabs. The readme mentions vertical layout, alignment, and styles. Usage: name each tab and its content. Right-click to convert to Nested Tabs.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-nested-tabs\"><h3>Nested Tabs</h3><p>Tabs whose panels can hold any unit. Shown when the Nested experiment is active, which it is by default. Usage: add a tab, then drop units into that panel.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-nested-accordion\"><h3>Nested Accordion</h3><p>An accordion whose panels hold any unit. Usage: add an item, then drop units inside it.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-nested-toggle\"><h3>Nested Toggle</h3><p>A toggle whose panels hold any unit. Usage: same as Nested Accordion.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-social\"><h3>Social Icons</h3><p>Links to social profiles. The readme mentions brand colors, shapes, and a grid. Usage: add each network and its URL.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-star-rating\"><h3>Star Rating</h3><p>Stars, including half stars in the readme. Controls include scale, rating, unmarked style, title, align, size, gap, and colors. Usage: set the rating.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-testimonial\"><h3>Testimonial</h3><p>A quote with the person’s details. Usage: enter the quote and the name.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-price-table\"><h3>Price Table</h3><p>A pricing box. Usage: set the plan name, price, features, and button.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-text-path\"><h3>Text Path</h3><p>Text that moves along a path. Defaults are the words “Canvasly” and a speed of 20. Usage: change Text and Speed. Speed is kept at 5 or higher.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-shortcode\"><h3>Shortcode</h3><p>Runs a WordPress shortcode you type. Default text is empty. Usage: paste a shortcode such as <code>[canvasly_lite_template id=\"42\"]</code> into the Shortcode field.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-html\"><h3>HTML</h3><p>Custom HTML, edited with the code editor when that feature is active. Usage: paste the markup into the HTML field.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-code\"><h3>Code</h3><p>Shows code in a preformatted block. Defaults are empty code and language “text”. Usage: paste the code and choose a language.</p></section>\n    </article>\n\n    <article id=\"lite-media\">\n      <h2>Media units</h2>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-image\"><h3>Image</h3><p class=\"meta\">Library category: media.</p><p>The readme lists image, size, link, lightbox, caption, alt text, object fit, object position, border, radius, shadow, opacity, spacing, position, and responsive settings. Usage: choose an image from the Media Library and fill in alt text.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-image-box\"><h3>Image Box</h3><p>An image with a title and text. Controls include image, alt, title, text, link, layout, alignment, width, spacing, radius, opacity, and colors. Usage: choose the image and write the title.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-gallery\"><h3>Gallery</h3><p>Several images. The readme mentions captions and hover effects. The editor can lay them out as grid, masonry, or justified. Usage: open the gallery control and choose images.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-carousel\"><h3>Image Carousel</h3><p>A slideshow of images. The readme mentions slides to show, fade, autoplay, and captions. Usage: choose the images and how many show at once.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-video\"><h3>Video</h3><p>YouTube, Vimeo, Dailymotion, VideoPress, or a file you host. The readme mentions overlay, lightbox, and aspect ratio. Usage: paste the video URL.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-audio\"><h3>Audio</h3><p>An audio player. A hosted file or an allowed oEmbed URL can be used. Usage: set the audio URL.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-soundcloud\"><h3>SoundCloud</h3><p>A SoundCloud player. Controls include URL, visual mode, height, autoplay, buying, liking, download, artwork, sharing, comments, play count, user, and player color. Usage: paste the SoundCloud URL.</p></section>\n    </article>\n\n    <article id=\"lite-content\">\n      <h2>Content units</h2>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-form\"><h3>Form</h3><p class=\"meta\">Library category: content.</p><p>A form on the page. reCAPTCHA runs only when Form reCAPTCHA is active and both keys are saved. With Pro, submissions can be stored under Canvasly → Submissions. Usage: add fields, set the button label, and choose where the form sends its message.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-read-more\"><h3>Read More</h3><p>A text link. Defaults are the words “Read More”, the URL “#”, and the same window. Usage: change Text, URL, and whether the link opens in the same window or a new one.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-rating\"><h3>Rating</h3><p>A rating made of icons. Controls include rating, max, label, icon, empty icon, color, size, and CSS class. Usage: set the score and the maximum.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-link-in-bio\"><h3>Link in Bio</h3><p>A title, subtitle, avatar, and a list of links. Usage: add the profile image and one link per line or item, depending on the control.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-site-nav\"><h3>Site Menu</h3><p>A WordPress menu that collapses under a chosen width. Defaults: no menu selected, horizontal layout, breakpoint 782, empty label. Usage: pick a menu from Appearance → Menus. The same output is the <code>canvasly_nav</code> shortcode.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-menu-anchor\"><h3>Menu Anchor</h3><p>A jump target. Default anchor id is <code>section-1</code>. You can also attach a menu. On the canvas the target is shown, and the page scrolls to that id. A sticky header can be cleared with the CSS custom property <code>--lb-anchor-scroll-offset</code>. Usage: set Anchor ID, then link a button or menu item to <code>#section-1</code>.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-collection-loop\"><h3>Collection Loop</h3><p>Lists posts, custom types, or terms. The readme describes a query builder, an item layout that is either inline or a saved template, grid or list, and pagination by numbers, previous and next, or load more. Active by default under Features. Usage: choose what to query, then design one item.</p></section>\n    </article>\n\n    <article id=\"lite-advanced\">\n      <h2>Advanced units</h2>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-embed\"><h3>Embed</h3><p>Embeds an allowed URL through WordPress oEmbed, with a cache. Usage: paste a single URL the site is allowed to embed.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-google-maps\"><h3>Google Maps</h3><p>A map. Defaults are an empty address, zoom 14, and height 320. With an API key and the Maps feature active, it uses the Embed API. Otherwise it uses the public iframe. Usage: type an address.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-sidebar\"><h3>Sidebar</h3><p>Prints a registered WordPress widget area. Usage: choose the sidebar.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-wp-widget\"><h3>WordPress Widget</h3><p>Prints one registered WordPress widget. Controls include widget, title, widget options, sidebar, and CSS class. Usage: choose the widget from the list.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-template\"><h3>Template</h3><p>Embeds a saved Canvasly template inside the page. Usage: pick the template. The same result is the shortcode <code>[canvasly_lite_template id=\"42\"]</code> and the WordPress block.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-component\"><h3>Component</h3><p>Inserts a reusable component. Components are saved from the right-click menu and edited as their own items. Usage: insert a component you already saved.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-flip-box\"><h3>Flip Box</h3><p>A card with a front and a back. Usage: set the front and back content, then view the page to see the flip.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-login\"><h3>Login</h3><p>The WordPress login form. Defaults are the title “Login”, the button “Log In”, and an empty redirect. Usage: set the title, button label, and where to send the person after login.</p></section>\n    </article>\n\n    <article id=\"pro-units\">\n      <h2>Canvasly Pro units</h2>\n      <p>These are in the library when Pro is running. Pro widgets use the content category unless noted. Theme units also use content. Payment Form and Payment Button use basic. Taxonomy Filter uses advanced.</p>\n\n      <h3>Content widgets</h3>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-share-buttons\"><h3>Share Buttons</h3><p>Buttons that share the page. Usage: add the unit and choose which networks to show.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-blockquote\"><h3>Blockquote</h3><p>A styled quotation. Usage: enter the quote.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-cta\"><h3>Call to Action</h3><p>A prompt with a button. Usage: write the message and the button link.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-animated-headline\"><h3>Animated Headline</h3><p>A headline that changes. Usage: enter the rotating phrases.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-countdown\"><h3>Countdown</h3><p>A timer toward a date. Usage: set the end date.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-slides\"><h3>Slides</h3><p>A slide deck. Usage: add slides and their content.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-nested-carousel\"><h3>Nested Carousel</h3><p>A carousel whose slides can hold other units. Usage: add a slide, then drop units into it.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-media-carousel\"><h3>Media Carousel</h3><p>A carousel of media items. Usage: choose the media.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-toc\"><h3>Table of Contents</h3><p>Links to headings on the page. Usage: place it where the list should appear.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-progress-tracker\"><h3>Progress Tracker</h3><p>Shows how far the visitor has scrolled or progressed. Usage: place it on a long page.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-hotspot\"><h3>Hotspot</h3><p>An image with clickable points. Usage: set the image and add hotspots.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-price-list\"><h3>Price List</h3><p>A list of named prices. Usage: add each item, name, and price.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-off-canvas\"><h3>Off-Canvas</h3><p>A panel that sits off the page until it is opened. Usage: put the panel content inside it and set how it opens.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-floating-buttons\"><h3>Floating Buttons</h3><p>Buttons that stay on the screen. Usage: add each button and its link.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-search\"><h3>Search</h3><p>A search box. Usage: place it in a header or sidebar area.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-lottie\"><h3>Lottie</h3><p>An animation file. Usage: choose the Lottie file.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-video-playlist\"><h3>Video Playlist</h3><p>Several videos in one list. Usage: add each video URL.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-code-highlight\"><h3>Code Highlight</h3><p>Code with highlighting. Usage: paste the code and choose the language.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-nav-menu\"><h3>Nav Menu</h3><p class=\"meta\">Library category: content.</p><p>A navigation menu from Pro. Usage: choose the menu to display.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-taxonomy-filter\"><h3>Taxonomy Filter</h3><p class=\"meta\">Library category: advanced.</p><p>Filters a loop by category or other taxonomy. Usage: place it with a Collection Loop and choose the taxonomy.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-payment-button\"><h3>Payment Button</h3><p class=\"meta\">Library category: basic.</p><p>A hosted PayPal or Stripe button. The readme says these stay hosted payment buttons. Usage: set the amount and the gateway. Site-wide tax and fees are under Canvasly → Payments.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-payment-form\"><h3>Payment Form</h3><p class=\"meta\">Library category: basic.</p><p>A form that checks out through the payment settings. Turn on Sales Tax, Bank Fee, or both. Each accepts a percent that is calculated and added to the total. Leave a percent blank to use the rate from Canvasly → Payments. Shipping is a fixed amount added to the total. Gateways offered under Canvasly → Payments appear as pay buttons at the bottom of the form. Each button uses a Payment Button on the same page with this form key.</p></section>\n\n      <h3>Theme units</h3>\n      <p>Use these inside Theme Builder templates so the text comes from the current page, post, or site.</p>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-site-logo\"><h3>Site Logo</h3><p>The site logo. Usage: place it in a header template.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-site-title\"><h3>Site Title</h3><p>The WordPress site title. Usage: place it in a header.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-site-tagline\"><h3>Site Tagline</h3><p>The WordPress tagline. Usage: place it near the site title.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-page-title\"><h3>Page Title</h3><p>The title of the current page. Usage: place it at the top of a single or page template.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-post-title\"><h3>Post Title</h3><p>The title of the current post. Usage: place it in a single template.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-post-excerpt\"><h3>Post Excerpt</h3><p>The excerpt of the current post. Usage: place it under the title.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-post-content\"><h3>Post Content</h3><p>The body of the current post. Usage: place it where the article should appear.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-post-info\"><h3>Post Info</h3><p>Meta such as date or author, as the unit’s controls allow. Usage: place it near the title.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-featured-image\"><h3>Featured Image</h3><p>The post’s featured image. Usage: place it in a single or archive item.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-author-box\"><h3>Author Box</h3><p>The author’s name and details. Usage: place it after the post.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-post-navigation\"><h3>Post Navigation</h3><p>Links to the previous and next post. Usage: place it at the end of a single template.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-post-comments\"><h3>Comments</h3><p>The WordPress comment area. Usage: place it under the post content.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-archive-title\"><h3>Archive Title</h3><p>The title of the current archive. Usage: place it at the top of an archive template.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-archive-posts\"><h3>Archive Posts</h3><p>The list of posts for the current archive. Usage: place it under the archive title.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-breadcrumbs\"><h3>Breadcrumbs</h3><p>A trail of links back to the home page. Usage: place it under the header.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-search-form\"><h3>Search Form</h3><p>The theme search form. Usage: place it in a header or 404 template.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-sitemap\"><h3>Sitemap</h3><p>A list of site content. Usage: place it on a sitemap page.</p></section>\n\n      <h3>Shop units</h3>\n      <p>These load only while WooCommerce is active. Product and product-archive templates use the theme locations. Cart, checkout, and My Account render WooCommerce’s own forms.</p>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-wc-products\"><h3>Products</h3><p>A product list. Usage: place it on a shop layout.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-wc-archive\"><h3>Archive Products</h3><p>Products for the current shop archive. Usage: place it in a product archive template.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-wc-title\"><h3>Product Title</h3><p>The current product’s name. Usage: place it on a product template.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-wc-image\"><h3>Product Image</h3><p>The current product’s image. Usage: place it beside the title.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-wc-price\"><h3>Product Price</h3><p>The current product’s price. Usage: place it under the title.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-wc-rating\"><h3>Product Rating</h3><p>The current product’s rating. Usage: place it near the price.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-wc-stock\"><h3>Product Stock</h3><p>Whether the product is in stock. Usage: place it near the add-to-cart button.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-wc-meta\"><h3>Product Meta</h3><p>SKU and categories from WooCommerce. Usage: place it under the product summary.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-wc-short\"><h3>Product Short Description</h3><p>The short description. Usage: place it above the full content.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-wc-content\"><h3>Product Content</h3><p>The long product description. Usage: place it in the product body.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-wc-tabs\"><h3>Product Data Tabs</h3><p>WooCommerce’s product tabs. Usage: place them under the summary.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-wc-cart\"><h3>Add To Cart</h3><p>The add-to-cart control from WooCommerce. Usage: place it with the price.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-wc-related\"><h3>Product Related</h3><p>Related products. Usage: place them after the product.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-wc-upsells\"><h3>Product Upsells</h3><p>Upsell products. Usage: place them on the product template.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-wc-notices\"><h3>Shop Notices</h3><p>WooCommerce notices such as “added to cart.” Usage: place them near the top of a shop template.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-wc-menu-cart\"><h3>Menu Cart</h3><p>A cart link for a menu or header. Usage: place it in the header.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-wc-cart-page\"><h3>Cart</h3><p>WooCommerce’s cart. Usage: place it on the cart template.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-wc-checkout\"><h3>Checkout</h3><p>WooCommerce’s checkout. Usage: place it on the checkout template.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-wc-account\"><h3>My Account</h3><p>WooCommerce’s account area. Usage: place it on the account template.</p></section>\n      <section class=\"unit\" id=\"unit-wc-summary\"><h3>Purchase Summary</h3><p>A summary after purchase. Usage: place it on the thank-you content.</p></section>\n    </article>\n\n    <article id=\"other-surfaces\">\n      <h2>Other things visitors and editors see</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Popups.</strong> A Popup template from Theme Builder is hidden in the footer and can open on load, scroll, click, exit, or inactivity. How often it shows uses a cookie set by the site. A link action can open a popup. A Canvasly form inside a popup still works.</li>\n        <li><strong>Editor notes.</strong> With Pro, a logged-in person who can edit the page can leave a note on a unit. Notes are not public comments and are not printed on the site.</li>\n        <li><strong>Saved templates.</strong> Stored as private content under Canvasly, with categories. Types include Page, Section, Container, Header, Footer, Single, Archive, Loop Item, and Floating Button. Pro adds the Theme Builder types.</li>\n        <li><strong>Maintenance.</strong> When maintenance or coming soon is on, visitors see the chosen template. Excluded roles still see the live site.</li>\n      </ul>\n    </article>\n    <nav class=\"card toc\" aria-label=\"Related\">\n      <h2>Related</h2>\n      <a href=\"/canvasly-shortcodes/\">Canvasly shortcodes</a>\n      <a href=\"/canvasly-settings/\">Canvasly settings</a>\n    </nav>"},"styles":{"base":{}},"children":[]}]}],"header":[],"footer":[],"settings":{"template":"default"}}]]></wp:meta_value>
		</wp:postmeta>
		<wp:postmeta>
			<wp:meta_key><![CDATA[_lb_document_version]]></wp:meta_key>
			<wp:meta_value><![CDATA[0.12.76]]></wp:meta_value>
		</wp:postmeta>
		<wp:postmeta>
			<wp:meta_key><![CDATA[_lb_document_updated]]></wp:meta_key>
			<wp:meta_value><![CDATA[2026-09-24 17:10:00]]></wp:meta_value>
		</wp:postmeta>
	</item>
	<item>
		<title>Canvasly AI connectivity and MCP</title>
		<link>https://canvasly.pro/canvasly-ai/</link>
		<pubDate>Thu, 24 Sep 2026 21:10:00 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[canvasly]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://canvasly.pro/?page_id=91007</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<!-- wp:html -->
<p class="brand"><a href="https://canvasly.pro"><img class="brand-logo" src="https://canvasly.pro/assets/canvasly-logo.png" alt="Canvasly. Design your pages visually." width="400" height="320" style="height:96px;width:auto;border-radius:12px"></a></p>
<!-- /wp:html -->

<!-- wp:html -->
<nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <ol>
        <li><a href="/canvasly-overview/">Canvasly</a></li>
        <li aria-current="page">AI and MCP</li>
      </ol>
    </nav>
<!-- /wp:html -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level": 1} -->
<h1 class="wp-block-heading">Canvasly AI connectivity and MCP</h1>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"className": "lede"} -->
<p class="lede">Canvasly Pro does not call an AI model to build pages. WordPress stores a provider API key and acts as a read-only Model Context Protocol (MCP) server. An outside agent, such as Cursor or Claude, reads layouts through MCP, then writes them back through a separate WordPress address. Canvasly itself never loads this. It exists only while Canvasly Pro is active.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"className": "meta"} -->
<p class="meta">Updated <time datetime="2026-09-22">September 22, 2026</time>. Covers Canvasly 0.12.76 and Canvasly Pro 0.6.3.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:html -->
<figure>
      <img src="assets/ai-flow.svg" alt="Four boxes in a row: save a provider key on AI Connection, enable MCP and generate a token, the agent reads the layout from WordPress, then a separate WordPress login saves the layout." width="880" height="220" decoding="async" fetchpriority="high">
      <figcaption>The key test and the MCP connection are not the save. Saving a page still needs a WordPress user who can edit layouts.</figcaption>
    </figure>
<!-- /wp:html -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">What you set up</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Open <strong>Canvasly → AI Connection</strong>. Only a site administrator can open it. Two stored settings are used:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list">
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Public connection:</strong> provider, model, Azure address when you use Azure, and whether MCP is on.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Secrets:</strong> the encrypted API key, and a hash of the MCP token. The plain key and the plain token are not kept in the page.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
</ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Both are removed if you uninstall Canvasly Pro. The editor never receives the API key. A status check reports only whether a key is stored.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Providers</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Only a provider on this list can be saved. An arbitrary web address is rejected, so this screen cannot be pointed at a private server. Every host must be HTTPS on port 443, with no username or password in the address. Localhost, addresses ending in <code>.local</code> or <code>.internal</code>, raw IP addresses, and lookalike hosts are rejected.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:html -->
<table>
        <thead><tr><th>Provider</th><th>Host used for the test</th><th>How the test sends the key</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>OpenAI</td><td>api.openai.com/v1</td><td>Bearer token</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Anthropic</td><td>api.anthropic.com/v1</td><td>x-api-key</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Gemini</td><td>generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta</td><td>x-goog-api-key</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Azure</td><td>Your own host under openai.azure.com, cognitiveservices.azure.com, or services.ai.azure.com</td><td>api-key</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Perplexity</td><td>api.perplexity.ai</td><td>Bearer token</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cursor</td><td>api.cursor.com/v1</td><td>Bearer token, and the test calls <code>/me</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Groq</td><td>api.groq.com/openai/v1</td><td>Bearer token</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mistral</td><td>api.mistral.ai/v1</td><td>Bearer token</td></tr>
          <tr><td>DeepSeek</td><td>api.deepseek.com</td><td>Bearer token</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
<!-- /wp:html -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>An Azure host must be a single name in front of one of those three endings. Add-ons may add providers, and those hosts still have to pass the same HTTPS checks.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">The API key and Test connection</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The key is encrypted before it is stored. A key must be 20 to 256 characters, with no spaces and no web address scheme such as <code>https://</code>. Leave the password field blank when you save if you want to keep the key already stored.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Test connection</strong> is the only time WordPress calls the provider. It checks the provider’s model list. Cursor is checked at <code>/me</code>. Azure is checked at <code>/openai/models?api-version=2024-10-21</code>. A successful response means the key was accepted. There is no chat, no completion, and no request that generates a layout. The model name you save is not sent to the provider.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Turn on MCP</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>WordPress is the MCP server. It does not forward those requests to OpenAI, Anthropic, or any other provider. The agent connects to your site:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:html -->
<pre class="example">POST /wp-json/canvasly-lite/v1/mcp</pre>
<!-- /wp:html -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The protocol version reported to the client is <code>2024-11-05</code>. The server name is <code>canvasly-lite</code>. This address is hidden from the public REST index.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The address stays closed until both of these are true: <strong>Enable MCP</strong> is checked, and a token exists. If MCP is on and there is no token, the screen warns you, and the address keeps answering with “forbidden” (HTTP 403).</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level": 3} -->
<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Token</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>A token starts with <code>lbai_</code> and then 48 hexadecimal characters. WordPress stores only a hash of it, a short hint of the first 8 characters, the administrator who created it, and the time. The full token is shown once, for two minutes. Generate a new one to replace it. Revoke deletes it. The person who owns the token must still be an administrator.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Send it on every MCP request in one of these ways:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:html -->
<pre class="example">Authorization: Bearer lbai_...</pre>
<!-- /wp:html -->

<!-- wp:html -->
<pre class="example">X-Canvasly-Lite-MCP-Token: lbai_...</pre>
<!-- /wp:html -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>A person who is already logged in to WordPress and can edit theme options can also call this address without the token. Each accepted call acts as the token’s owner, and that person must be allowed to edit layouts. After 60 calls in a minute, further calls are refused until the minute passes.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">What the agent can read</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>MCP can list and read layouts. It cannot create, update, or delete them.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:html -->
<table>
        <thead><tr><th>Request</th><th>What comes back</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>initialize</td><td>Protocol version, server info, and a note that the tools are read-only.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>ping</td><td>An empty result.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>tools/list and tools/call</td><td>The three tools below.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>resources/list</td><td>One entry for each Canvasly page this person can edit.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>resources/read</td><td>The layout for an address like <code>canvasly-lite://document/123</code>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>prompts/list</td><td>Always empty. No prompts are registered.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
<!-- /wp:html -->

<!-- wp:html -->
<table>
        <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it returns</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>list_layout_documents</td><td>Up to 50 posts or pages that store a Canvasly layout and that this person can edit. An optional title search narrows the list.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>get_layout_document</td><td>One page: its number, title, type, status, and layout.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>get_layout_schema</td><td>The catalog of units and shared settings, plus the page layout when a page number is set. Compact results are the default. Empty and default settings are left out so the tree stays small. Saving fills those defaults back in.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
<!-- /wp:html -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How the agent saves a page</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Writes do not go through MCP. They use the layout routes below. The MCP token proves who is calling MCP. It does not, by itself, allow the save. The agent also needs a WordPress login, or another WordPress credential, that can edit layouts.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:html -->
<table>
        <thead><tr><th>Address</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>GET /wp-json/canvasly-lite/v1/layout-schema</td><td>The catalog of units. Requires permission to edit theme options.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>GET /wp-json/canvasly-lite/v1/layout-schema/{id}</td><td>The catalog plus the layout of that page.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>POST /wp-json/canvasly-lite/v1/layout-schema/{id}</td><td>Checks the layout and saves it. Add <code>render=1</code> to also receive the HTML.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>POST /wp-json/canvasly-lite/v1/layout-schema/render</td><td>Checks the layout and returns HTML without saving.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
<!-- /wp:html -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The catalog id is <code>canvasly-lite.layout.v1</code>. A layout is a version, page settings, and a list of units. Each unit has an id, a type, settings, children, and an optional slot. The agent is told to:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list">
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Use only unit types from the catalog. A type that accepts children may contain other units.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>When a type uses slots, set the child’s slot to the matching item id.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Use only setting names that belong to that unit or to the shared settings. Unknown names are dropped on save.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Apply a global class with <code>global_class</code>, using names that already exist, separated by spaces.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Reuse a saved component with type <code>component</code> and its component id.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Keep existing unit ids. Leave the id off, or start a new one with <code>n_</code>. The save step assigns an id if one is missing.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Send the layout to the save address to keep it, or to the render address to preview HTML. Do not invent the page’s HTML. Canvasly turns the layout into the page.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
</ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>A save can be the full catalog response, an object that contains a <code>document</code>, or an object that has a <code>root</code> list.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">The working loop</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list {"ordered":true} -->
<ol class="wp-block-list">
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>An administrator saves a provider and API key, then uses Test connection. That only proves the provider accepts the key.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>The administrator turns on MCP and generates a token. The token is stored in the agent, not in the page.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>The agent sends JSON-RPC to <code>/wp-json/canvasly-lite/v1/mcp</code> with the bearer token.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>It reads the catalog and, if needed, one page. It edits that layout with whatever model the agent is already using.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>It posts the layout to <code>/wp-json/canvasly-lite/v1/layout-schema/{page number}</code> with WordPress credentials that can edit the page. Canvasly checks it, saves it, and the public page is drawn from that layout.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
</ol>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The provider key stored in WordPress is not part of that loop. Pro does not send the layout, the prompt, or the model id to OpenAI, Anthropic, Cursor, or the other listed hosts, except for the model-list check used by Test connection.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Limits that stay in place</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list">
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Canvasly by itself does not register these addresses. They exist only while the Pro AI module is active.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>The MCP address and the AI Connection addresses are left out of the public REST index.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>MCP tools cannot create, update, or delete layouts.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Reading a page still checks that this person may edit that page.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Saves use the same checks and the same page renderer as the public site.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Provider requests do not follow redirects, require a valid certificate, and refuse any address that is not on the list.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>MCP is limited to 60 calls per person per minute.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
</ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Related</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list">
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><a href="/canvasly-settings/">Canvasly settings</a></li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><a href="/canvasly-faq/">Common questions</a></li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
</ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->]]></content:encoded>
